1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,240
 In this lesson, we're going to learn how to extend interfaces and TypeScript.

2
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:11,700
 So extending interfaces allows us to build on existing object structures instead of repeating

3
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:16,600
 ourselves, which is essential for writing clean and scalable code.

4
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:22,000
 This feature is used heavily in frameworks, libraries, and large code bases.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
 Let's start with a common problem.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,500
 We often have objects that share some properties, but also have their own unique fields.

7
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:36,000
 With its extension, we'd have to repeat the same properties over and over.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
 So let's zoom in space like this and do interface, we've seen this before.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 I'll be user, the ID of number, and name of string.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 So this interface defines the basic structure of our user object.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 Now let's create a more specific type of user.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
 We do that by adding interface.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 Let's say admin user, and we use the keyword extends, extends, and then the type.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
 So extends our user type.

15
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 And then we specify the property that we want to append, add onto this user object.

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 Let's say we want to have a role object, a role property, we can set it to admin.

17
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
 So this extends keywords, allows our admin user to inherit all the properties from user,

18
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
 and it also adds its own.

19
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 Okay, so that means we can create an object like this.

20
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
 We could do a const, admin user, we can specify it as our admin user,

21
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
 and then we specify our ID property, our name, let's say Alice,

22
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 and then we have this extra property of role, which can then set to admin,

23
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,000
 or the live value.

24
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:09,000
 This object must satisfy both the user and the admin user interfaces when they're combined.

25
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 Now an interface can extend multiple interfaces.

26
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 So we have our user here, now let's just remove this code for moments,

27
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
 and we're going to define another interface.

28
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,000
 This is has permissions.

29
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 Let's give it the permissions property.

30
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
 This is going to be an array of strings.

31
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 Now we're going to have another interface.

32
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 This is going to be a super admin interface, super admin, which extends both the user,

33
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 interface, and also the has permissions interface, both these together.

34
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 Now we can define the role and say role is super admin.

35
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:06,000
 Let's let's us to combine multiple behaviors of two interfaces into one single interface.

36
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,000
 Let's now have a look at a real-world example that you often see in APIs.

37
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 We're going to define an interface, or say base response.

38
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
 This is going to have a status of number.

39
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:31,000
 And now let's define another interface, perhaps user response.

40
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 This is going to extends our base response.

41
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:43,000
 And on this we're going to now define our data, as an object,

42
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 as an ID of number, then perhaps a name of string.

43
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
 Now this pattern keeps our APIs consistent while allowing different payloads.

44
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 Now that we've defined our user response,

45
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 we could define other responses for other entities, perhaps, for example customer response or product response.

46
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,000
 And because he's now extended base response, all of them are going to inherit the status,

47
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,000
 and we're going to have the same shape with our data ID to keep things consistent.

